<body>


<div class="wrap">
<header>
  <span class="tag" style="justify-self:start">n8n · AI · Operations &amp; Logistics</span>
  <h1>Workflows that catch operational problems <em>before they cost a customer</em></h1>
  <p class="who"><b>Federico Acevedo</b> · Logistics Planning (final year) · Google Project Management Certificate · 10+ years coordinating daily field operations</p>
  <div class="principles">
    <div class="pr"><b>Code decides</b><span>Rules, calculations, links and priorities live in code. The AI only explains or writes, from facts it is given.</span></div>
    <div class="pr"><b>Nothing invented</b><span>A final check blocks any sentence with a number, date or ID that is not in the data.</span></div>
    <div class="pr"><b>Tested before delivery</b><span>Synthetic data with problems planted on purpose, an answer key written first, and an independent re-implementation to compare.</span></div>
  </div>
  <nav aria-label="Cases"><a href="#case-3">AI Logistics Control Tower</a><a href="#case-1">Daily Ops Exceptions Monitor</a><a href="#case-2">AI Order-Status Replies</a></nav>
</header>

<!-- CASE 3 -->
<section class="case" id="case-3">
  <span class="tag" style="justify-self:start">Case 3</span>
  <h2>AI Logistics Control Tower: <em>what to look at this week</em></h2>
  <div class="strip">
    <img src="img/C3_1_problem.png" alt="Problem: fleet, stock and orders live on three screens" loading="lazy">
    <img src="img/C3_2_solution.png" alt="Solution: code calculates and ranks, AI explains" loading="lazy">
    <img src="img/C3_3_result.png" alt="Result: van CAM-02 delay per stop grows from 4.9 to 12.4 minutes; 36 of 36 findings" loading="lazy">
  </div>
  <div class="cols">
    <div class="block">
      <h3>The problem</h3>
      <p>A distributor with its own vans has three people on three screens: routes, stock and orders. The costly problems build up slowly and cross those lines: a van a little later every week, a summer product with a reorder point set for winter, a supplier whose lead time swings.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top:10px">What it does</h3>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>Reads post-trip GPS from two providers through adapters. A quality gate stops the analysis if too few stops match a customer.</li>
        <li>Applies fleet rules: long stops, late starts, delay that builds up, low km per hour, worsening week by week, service due by km.</li>
        <li>Forecasts demand per product, testing up to 12 methods on the last 26 weeks, then computes safety stock, reorder point, days of cover and forecast bias.</li>
        <li>Links findings across areas (van → trip → order → customer) and ranks them by severity, customer impact and urgency.</li>
        <li>Three AI specialists explain each finding; the report skeleton, numbers and order come from code. A web page answers follow-up questions through one agent with four code tools.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="who-does"><span class="chip code">code: rules, forecast, scoring</span><span class="chip">AI: explains, routes questions</span><span class="chip">code: final check</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="block">
      <h3>How it was tested</h3>
      <div class="tbl"><table>
        <tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Result</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Findings equal to the answer key</td><td class="num">36 / 36</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Planted problems found</td><td class="num">7 / 7</td></tr>
        <tr><td>False positives</td><td class="num">0</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Invented numbers or IDs</td><td class="num">0</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Forecast and stock math vs. independent re-implementation</td><td class="num">100%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>GPS provider B vs. provider A (same stops, ±2 min)</td><td class="num">66 / 66</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Questions answered correctly (final run)</td><td class="num">6 / 6</td></tr>
      </table></div>
      <p class="muted" style="font-size:15px">8 weeks of synthetic operation: 10 vehicles, 100 wholesale customers, ~4,900 order lines, 60 products with 3 years of weekly sales.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top:10px">Found and fixed during the build</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>A stable pattern looked like "getting worse" by chance (1 time in 6): worsening now needs 20% growth, measured per stop.</li>
        <li>The forecast-drift rule flagged 46 of 60 products on lumpy recent orders: replaced by a tracking signal (1 product).</li>
        <li>The agent listed a customer who had only sent queries as "affected": the tool now returns the exact list, written by code.</li>
        <li>A JSON reply was cut at the output cap because the model's reasoning counts inside it: cap and pauses measured and raised.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="stack">n8n (self-hosted, Docker) · AI Agent with code tools · Groq (swappable model) · Google Sheets · Gmail · Telegram · web page served by n8n · JavaScript</p>
</section>

<!-- CASE 1 -->
<section class="case" id="case-1">
  <span class="tag" style="justify-self:start">Case 1</span>
  <h2>Daily Ops Exceptions Monitor: <em>only what fails, every morning</em></h2>
  <div class="strip">
    <img src="img/C1_1_problem.png" alt="Problem: errors between orders, invoices and stock are found too late" loading="lazy">
    <img src="img/C1_2_solution.png" alt="Solution: one n8n workflow with 10 rules checks everything every morning" loading="lazy">
    <img src="img/C1_3_result.png" alt="Result: 25 of 25 planted errors found, 0 false alarms" loading="lazy">
  </div>
  <div class="cols">
    <div class="block">
      <h3>The problem</h3>
      <p>Someone checks orders, supplier invoices, shipments and stock by hand every day. Overbilling, duplicate invoices and stuck parcels still slip through, and are found when they already cost money.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top:10px">What it does</h3>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>Reads the four sources and a config tab with every threshold (nothing hard-coded).</li>
        <li>Runs 10 rules: quantity or price invoiced vs. agreed, duplicate invoices, invoice without order, shipped without tracking after 24 h, in transit with no carrier events, carrier exceptions, low, mismatched or negative stock.</li>
        <li>Sends a short report with only the exceptions, each with its rule, severity, detail and suggested action. An error workflow reports failures.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="who-does"><span class="chip code">code: all 10 rules</span><span class="chip">no AI needed</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="block">
      <h3>How it was tested</h3>
      <div class="tbl"><table>
        <tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Result</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Planted errors found</td><td class="num">25 / 25</td></tr>
        <tr><td>False alarms</td><td class="num">0</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Edge cases just under a threshold, correctly not flagged</td><td class="num">4 / 4</td></tr>
      </table></div>
      <p class="muted" style="font-size:15px">Synthetic data: 609 orders (1,032 lines), 954 invoices, 560 shipments, 60 products. Errors planted on purpose, with the expected result written down before the test.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="stack">n8n (self-hosted, Docker) · Google Sheets · email report · JavaScript</p>
</section>

<!-- CASE 2 -->
<section class="case" id="case-2">
  <span class="tag" style="justify-self:start">Case 2</span>
  <h2>AI Order-Status Replies: <em>the code decides, the AI only writes</em></h2>
  <div class="strip">
    <img src="img/C2_1_problem.png" alt="Problem: where is my order emails answered by hand all day" loading="lazy">
    <img src="img/C2_2_solution.png" alt="Solution: AI reads, code looks up the order and applies 8 rules, AI writes, check, reply or escalate" loading="lazy">
    <img src="img/C2_3_result.png" alt="Result: 40 of 40 correct decisions, 0 invented data, 2 of 2 manipulation attempts with no effect" loading="lazy">
  </div>
  <div class="cols">
    <div class="block">
      <h3>The problem</h3>
      <p>Most support emails ask the same thing: where is my order. Someone looks up the order, the tracking and the carrier, then types the reply, all day.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top:10px">What it does</h3>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>The AI reads each email and extracts the intent; code extracts the order number and recalls the sender's last 5 emails for follow-ups.</li>
        <li>Code looks up the real order and shipment and applies 8 rules: reply with the status, ask for a missing order number, or escalate (refunds, damage, several orders, unknown order, sender not matching the customer, order with a known problem).</li>
        <li>The AI writes the reply from verified facts; a check blocks any order number, tracking code, date, email or link that is not in the data before it is sent in the same thread.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="who-does"><span class="chip">AI: reads, writes</span><span class="chip code">code: lookup, 8 rules, memory, check</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="block">
      <h3>How it was tested</h3>
      <div class="tbl"><table>
        <tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Result</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Correct decisions (reply or escalate)</td><td class="num">40 / 40</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Invented data in replies</td><td class="num">0</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Follow-ups resolved from memory</td><td class="num">3 / 3</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Manipulation attempts with no effect</td><td class="num">2 / 2</td></tr>
      </table></div>
      <p class="muted" style="font-size:15px">40 synthetic emails from 322 customers, including follow-ups without an order number, privacy cases and prompt-injection attempts.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="stack">n8n (self-hosted, Docker) · LLM via swappable sub-node (Groq) · Gmail · Google Sheets · Telegram (escalations) · JavaScript</p>
</section>

<footer>
  <p>All three projects are portfolio demos built on synthetic data for a fictional parts distributor. No real client data was used.</p>
